  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Operator's HUD",
    "overview": "The site is a heads-up display that the visitor operates, not a page they read past. They steer with a compass, and panels lock on as they arrive. Rows read like log entries, and readouts report Neil's work as live system data. Every HUD device has a job: brackets frame what matters, notches mark what's active, and the scan sweep points at the one thing to do next. The cyberpunk feel comes from precision and response, not from neon saturation.\n\nThe system is restrained at rest and alive on interaction. Surfaces are flat canvas and surface tones divided by 1–2px lines. Teal is the only signal color, and motion answers the visitor:\n- a row nudges toward the cursor;\n- a badge flips;\n- the hologram card tilts toward a finger.\n\nThe one big orchestrated moment is the first-visit loading screen; after that, effects stay small.\n\nIt works in both themes. Dark mode is a pure black void with a bright signal teal; light mode is a cool instrument-panel grey with a deep teal. Neither theme is an afterthought.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One signal color (teal) on neutral canvas and surface tones.",
      "Corner brackets, cut corners and heavy-left-edge rows instead of filled cards and soft shadows.",
      "A condensed display face (Saira Condensed) for titles and short caps labels; IBM Plex Sans for everything else.",
      "Motion is feedback (lock-on, wipe, scan, flip, tilt), always with a settled reduced-motion state.",
      "No neon palettes, no glowing body text, no monospace costume."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Signal Rule",
        "body": "Teal is the only accent. Never add a second accent hue (no magenta, matrix green or neon cyan) for decoration. The amber series color exists only inside the career timeline chart.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Token-Only Rule",
        "body": "Tailwind's default palette is removed (`--color-*: initial`). Every color comes from these tokens, and new tints are `color-mix()` of them, never new hex values.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Caps-for-Labels-Only Rule",
        "body": "Uppercase is for short HUD labels of one to three words. Headings, links and sentences stay in Title Case or sentence case, never all caps.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No-Costume-Mono Rule",
        "body": "There is no monospace face. Numbers and dates use Plex with `tabular-nums`. Don't add a monospace font to look technical.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Glow-Means-Aimed Rule",
        "body": "Glow appears only on the thing the visitor is currently aiming at. It never decorates resting cards, text or buttons.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do use Signal Teal as the only accent, and mix tints from it with `color-mix()`.",
      "Do frame important content with corner brackets, notches and frame lines instead of filled, shadowed cards.",
      "Do give every animation a settled final state under `prefers-reduced-motion` (content is fully visible and nothing hides behind motion).",
      "Do make motion answer the visitor (hover, focus, drag, press), and keep idle motion to the hologram card's sway and the loader.",
      "Do design and check both themes, with AA contrast for every text and background pair.",
      "Do keep labels in Title Case and reserve caps for short HUD labels."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't add neon magenta, matrix green or a second accent hue.",
      "Don't put glow or `text-shadow` on body text, headings or resting elements. Glow means \"aimed\".",
      "Don't add a monospace font for a technical look. Use `tabular-nums` for figures.",
      "Don't add constant glitching or CRT flicker outside the loading screen.",
      "Don't use heavy blur or frosted-glass panels; the hologram card owns the holographic look.",
      "Don't build a dark-only effect. Every treatment must work in light mode too.",
      "Don't round HUD elements; radii are for the few soft controls only."
    ]
  }
}
